/// EXPECTED TO WORK — currently DECLINES. (iterators stress test, 2026-07-14)
///
/// The canonical lazy THUNK: a closure `Unit -> T` stored in a heap sum variant, pulled back out
/// and called. This is the heart of a lazy pull-iterator (`Iter a = Susp(Unit -> Option (a, Iter a))`
/// in the iterators proposal): laziness in a strict language needs a delayed computation, and the
/// idiomatic delay is a nullary/`Unit`-parameter thunk.
///
/// ACTUAL: `cdz check` is CLEAN, but `cdz compile` DECLINES with
///   error: a closure's parameter type has no machine representation
/// at the `mk` line (the `fn(u) => ...` boxed into `Thunk.Susp`).
///
/// ROOT CAUSE (root-caused during the stress test):
///   backend/wasm/lir.rs `valtype_of`: `Ty::Unit => None` — a Unit-typed value has no machine
///   valtype (it is zero-width). lower.rs (~8044) requires EVERY boxed-closure parameter to have a
///   `valtype_of`, so a `Unit`-param closure that becomes a heap resource is declined at that guard.
///
/// KEY DISCRIMINATORS (all verified):
///   - A `Unit`-param closure called DIRECTLY or passed as a plain ARG works fine (returns 42) —
///     only BOXING it into a sum variant (making it a runtime resource) trips the guard.
///   - The same sum with an `Int64`-param closure works and runs (a non-Unit param has a valtype).
///   - A `Unit`-RETURNING boxed closure `(-> Int64 Unit)` works — only Unit in PARAMETER position
///     at the closure boundary is the gap.
///
/// SUGGESTED FIX: give `Unit` a zero-information machine slot at the closure/resource boundary
///   (either elide a Unit param slot entirely in the boxed-closure calling convention, or lower it
///   to a placeholder i32 that is never read). Unit already flows fine as a top-level fn param and as
///   a closure result; the boxed-closure PARAMETER path is the one place it is rejected.
///
/// This unblocks the ideal thunk-based lazy `Iter` (see the iterators proposal / src/iter.cdz, which
/// currently uses a reified/defunctionalized encoding to avoid this wall).

type Thunk =
  | Susp(Unit -> Int64)

def force(t: Thunk) = match t with
  | Thunk.Susp(f) => f(unit)

def mk() = Thunk.Susp(fn(u) => 42)

@test
def force-runs-the-thunk() = if force(mk()) == 42 then unit else trap("thunk should force to 42")

def main() = force(mk())
export { main }
